# MOFs in the time domain

## abstract
Many of the proposed applications of metal-organic framework (MOF) materials may fail to materialize if the community does not fully address the difficult fundamental work needed to map out the 'time gap' in the literature - that is, the lack of investigation into the time-dependent behaviours of MOFs as opposed to equilibrium or steady-state properties. Although there are a range of excellent investigations into MOF dynamics and time-dependent phenomena, these works represent only a tiny fraction of the vast number of MOF studies. This Review provides an overview of current research into the temporal evolution of MOF structures and properties by analysing the time-resolved experimental techniques that can be used to monitor such behaviours. We focus on innovative techniques, while also discussing older methods often used in other chemical systems. Four areas are examined: MOF formation, guest motion, electron motion and framework motion. In each area, we highlight the disparity between the relatively small amount of (published) research on key time-dependent phenomena and the enormous scope for acquiring the wider and deeper understanding that is essential for the future of the field.

Daniel J. Cerasale, Dominic C. Ward and Timothy L. Easun ✉
Abstract | Many of the proposed applications of metal–organic framework (MOF) materials may fail to materialize if the community does not fully address the difficult fundamental work needed to map out the ‘time gap’ in the literature — that is, the lack of investigation into the time-​dependent behaviours of MOFs as opposed to equilibrium or steady-​state properties. Although there are a range of excellent investigations into MOF dynamics and time-​dependent phenomena, these works represent only a tiny fraction of the vast number of MOF studies. This Review provides an overview of current research into the temporal evolution of MOF structures and properties by analysing the time-​resolved experimental techniques that can be used to monitor such behaviours. We focus on innovative techniques, while also discussing older methods often used in other chemical systems. Four areas are examined: MOF formation, guest motion, electron motion and framework motion. In each area, we highlight the disparity between the relatively small amount of (published) research on key time-​dependent phenomena and the enormous scope for acquiring the wider and deeper understanding that is essential for the future of the field.
New metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are frequently being added to the literature, but the days of simply reporting a static, pretty MOF structure are largely behind us. Today’s most exciting studies describe the time-​dependent phenomena that more accurately represent the real state of a MOF crystal or particle and of the guests within. Investigations into dynamic pheno­ mena in chemistry and physics, such as nanofluidics1, ion or electron conduction2,3 and photochemistry4,5, have been enabled by the highly crystalline nature of most MOFs, studied with techniques such as $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -​ray or neutron diffraction6,7, as well as spectroscopy8 and microscopy methods9. As the variation in linker size, shape and properties is limited by only the ingenuity of synthetic chemists, and many different metal ions can be combined with these linkers, the accessible dynamic chemical phase space afforded by MOFs is vast10–13.
The high degree of intrinsic structural variation gives rise to one of the most appealing features of MOFs: highly tunable framework properties, such as pore size, shape, surface area and chemistry. This, in turn, allows for their targeted use in various applications, including gas storage and separation14–16, photocatalysis17,18, drug delivery19–22, sensing23–25 and fuel cells26,27.
The term ‘soft porous crystals’, coined in 2009 by Kitagawa and co-​workers28, describes a subclass of MOF that display some degree of framework flexibility while maintaining structural integrity throughout reversible framework changes caused by stimuli, including temperature change29, pressure30, guest variation31 or light32.
These ‘flexible’ MOFs might appear to be considerably less abundant in crystal structure databases than their rigid counterparts, but it is likely that the flexibility limits of most reported frameworks have not been investigated33.
Understanding the fundamental physics and chemistry that govern the mechanisms of structural transfomations in flexible frameworks is key to the successful development of applications. Quantum chemistry calculations and molecular dynamics/grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations can provide some mechanistic insight34,35 but are often limited to largely idealized and spatially constrained fragments of MOF crystals. Real MOF samples frequently contain defects and display phenomena that are dependent on crystal size36,37, which can only be examined by taking time-​resolved experimental measurements of the system during the dynamic events. By monitoring at time intervals that are sufficiently short to capture the dynamic phenomena in question, key information on transiently accessed, non-​equilibrium states can be obtained. In turn, this information enables the nature of the potential energy surface that describes the framework during its contortions to be pieced together. The ultimate goal is to realize the rational design of flexible $\ensuremath{{}^{\circ}}4\ensuremath{\mathrm{D}}$ frameworks’ with deliberate and predictable time-​dependent properties — a goal that was set out by Kaskel and colleagues in 2020 (ref.38) and framed in conceptual terms. However, obtaining detailed experimental information with which to experimentally apply the conceptual framework is still very difficult.
In this Review, we assess the state of experimental MOF research in the time domain, using a technique $\rightarrow$ phenomenon $\rightarrow$ framework approach. By putting the techniques at the forefront of the discussion, we hope to demonstrate their utility in the study of time-​resolved MOF dynamics and to highlight several newer or less well known techniques (Fig. 1; Table 1). We divide the field into four subsections on the basis of the type of motional behaviour investigated. In one sense, the most dynamic of behaviours is the synthetic process itself. We thus begin by describing time-​resolved techniques that monitor the MOF synthesis process, followed by discussion of the time-​resolved study of guest translational motion throughout MOF pores. We then consider the phenomenon of electron motion, describing charge transfer and conductivity in MOFs, before examining studies on framework motion.
The studies described below represent important progress in the ongoing investigation of MOFs in the time domain, but constitute just a tiny fraction of the vast MOF literature. Given the wealth of important information that is accessible by considering dynamic behaviours of MOFs, if they are to be fully exploited for their scientific and commercial potential, this situation needs to change.
## MOF formation
The construction of a MOF begins on the nanoscale, with metal–ligand complex formation, leading up to macro­ scale particle growth and bulk material production. MOF formation relies on the ultrafast bond-​breaking and bond-​making processes of self-​assembly and supramolecular chemistry, which ultimately produce crystal growth on timescales of seconds to days. No single technique can interrogate the many species that exist during MOF synthesis on timescales from femto­seconds to days, but each process during framework construction affects the product that forms and its properties.
Synthetic method, particle size, morphology, defect content, surface chemistry and phase behaviour all influence the final material, and all stem from the formation process39–50. Understanding MOF formation is key to unlocking function-​led design of MOFs; the breadth of this challenge is summarized in Fig. 2.
The use of in  situ, time-​resolved techniques to study MOF formation has been reviewed extensively51. However, this is a fast-​moving area, and we therefore discuss cutting-​edge experimental techniques in the field as well as more established techniques.
$X$ -​ray scattering methods (timescale: seconds to hours). Diffraction methods provide information on late-​stage MOF formation when crystallites of sufficient size and structural order to diffract are formed. The most common model applied to crystallization data is the Gualtieri model, which describes the process using parameters of nucleation rate and crystal growth rate52. The faster acquisition time of energy-​dispersive X-​ray diffraction (EDXRD) resulted in it being the method of choice53,54, but developments in detector technology have made in situ angular-​dispersive X-​ray diffraction (ADXRD) a viable alternative55–57.
A prime example of the utility and impact of in situ EDXRD was a 2014 report on UiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr})$ formation58. In situ EDXRD was used to study the crystallization kinetics of UiO- $66(\mathrm{Zr})$ for the first time, with a 30-​s time resolution. Three key experimental observations were made: addition of water increases the crystallization rate constant and yield; addition of HCl does the same but to a lesser extent; and the use of $\mathrm{\mathrm{ZrOCl}}_{_{2}}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{_{2}}\mathrm{O}$ instead of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ as the precursor slows crystallization but affords a higher product yield. It was subsequently reported that changing linker length and functionalization had a temperature-​dependent impact on the crystallization rate of the analogous isoreticular frameworks owing to differing linker solubilities in the reaction59.
Not all frameworks form as single-​phase, phase-​pure materials60–62. Cutting-​edge ADXRD experiments at the Diamond Light Source synchrotron (UK), with data collected every 4 s, enabled a particularly notable study of a complex system63. Using this technique, it was possible to describe the energetics of lithium tartrate MOF crystallization and quantify the rate constants and activation barriers for formation, dissolution and conversion of each component in the energy landscape. Crystallization was governed by an equilibrium between intermediate species, with the inference being that changes in ligand conformation might have a rate-​limiting role in the formation of MOFs with flexible linkers. In more rigid examples, such as ZIF-8, ADXRD has been used to examine the impact of metal ion concentration on nucleation and crystal growth rates64; the authors derived a model whereby prior to ZIF-8 network assembly, a pre-​equilibrium exists between metastable intermediate clusters with varying $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ -​to-​mIM– (where mIM– is 2-​methylimidazolate) ratios and degrees of protonation. This pre-​equilibrium is concentration depen­ dent and determines the rate of crystallization, meaning that concentration can be used as a handle to directly control particle size.
X-​ray diffraction methods are limited by the need for diffracting particles. Small-​angle X-​ray scattering (SAXS), however, can provide insight on the formation and behaviour of pre-​diffraction and amorphous species65,66. Synchrotron time-​resolved SAXS with a 100-​ms time resolution was used to monitor the nucleation, growth and crystallization of ZIF-8 encapsulated with bovine serum albumin $(\mathrm{BSA}@Z\mathrm{IF}-8)$ ) in a continuous-​flow synthesis67. SAXS data reported on the growth of non-​diffracting species over the first $3\mathrm{min}$ of the synthesis (on length scales of up to $35\mathrm{nm}$ ), after which crystalline ${\mathrm{BSA@ZIF}}\cdot8$ formation began to dominate, as observed by the growth of the (110) Bragg diffraction peak and loss of amorphous scattering. SAXS has also been used to monitor the homogeneous nucleation and early crystal-​growth events occurring during ZIF-8 formation68, to investigate the kinetics of multi­ phase growth of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -​MIL-53 (ref.69) and to study the entire formation mechanism of ZIF-71 nanocrystals70.
Many MOFs have been synthesized by mechanically grinding dry reagents together, in the absence of solvent — a process known as mechanosynthesis — a particularly green method that is on the rise71. In situ measurements of mechanochemical MOF synthesis are extremely challenging given the nature of the method, but X-​ray and spectroscopic methods have been used in a few cases72–74.
The examples in this section demonstrate the utility of X-​ray methods for the mechanistic analysis of MOF formation but are ultimately limited in their scope by requiring particles large enough to scatter (typically $_{1-100\mathrm{nm}}$ for $\mathrm{SAXS})^{65}$ or diffract (typically ${>}5\mathrm{-}10\mathrm{nm}$ for powder diffraction patterns and ${\tt>}10{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ for single-​crystal structure solutions)75,76. Additionally, despite the atomistic information provided by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -​ray crystallography, X-​ray techniques measure spatially averaged data, providing bulk-​average information with local structural irregularities invisible to these methods.
Microscopy (timescale: seconds to minutes). Microscopy techniques can be used to interrogate surface pheno­ mena and local structural changes instead of spatially averaged structural features77,78.
The growth of MOF-5 was monitored by taking scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images with a time resolution of the order of hours62, revealing that MOF-5 nucleation does not occur in the solution, but inside $\mathrm{Zn}_{5}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{-}1,4\cdot$ -​BDC (where BDC is 1,4-​benzenedicarboxylate) particles. The building units for the construction of MOF-5 cubes were identified as small MOF-5 crystallites, which self-​assemble into porous microcubes. These microcubes then undergo surface recrystallization and reversed crystal growth to yield cubic single crystals of MOF-5. This study highlights how in situ SEM can relate the MOF formation mechanism to MOF morphology and, in turn, to its physicochemical properties.
Single-​molecule atomic-​resolution real-​time electron microscopic (SMART-​EM) video imaging is a TEM technique with extremely high spatial resolution. It has been used to investigate early-​stage MOF nucleation79, taking images at a rate of two frames per second. The authors identified two prenucleation clusters involved in the formation of MOF-2 and MOF-5, frameworks that can be preferentially formed from the same reaction mixture when heated at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively. The shape of the prenucleation clusters differs at each temperature, with cube-​like prenucleation clusters leading to MOF-5 formation at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 2D squares leading to MOF-2 formation at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , reflecting the final cubic and square lattices of the product MOFs, respectively. The difference in the prenucleation cluster morphology suggests that MOF-2/5 bifurcation occurs early, in the prenucleation cluster stage. Prior to this study, MOF-2 and MOF-5 prenucleation clusters had been identified only by mass spectrometry. Earlier in situ studies using light scattering could only reveal small crystallites, lacking the spatial resolution to image the prenucleation clusters80.
Liquid-​cell transmission electron microscopy (LCTEM) allows for the direct probing of MOF solution-​phase dynamics and solid-​phase growth. With a time resolution of the order of seconds, in situ LCTEM was used to study the nucleation and crystal growth of ZIF-8 (ref.81). LCTEM images showed that by tuning the metal-​to-​ligand ratio, the crystal size can be controlled. The nucleation process was also shown to be limited by local depletion of monomers, whereas growth is a surface-​limited process.
The crystal growth process of ZIF-8 has been further probed using in situ atomic force microscopy $(\mathrm{AFM})^{82}$ . With a time resolution of minutes and a spatial resolution of the order of nanometres, growth was shown to occur by a ‘growth and spread’ mechanism, eventually forming stable surface steps of the enclosed framework structure. More recently, the kinetics of both nucleation and crystal growth of HKUST-1 thin films were investigated using in situ liquid-​phase AFM (LPAFM) with a time resolution of $15\mathrm{min}$ (ref.83). At $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ initial nucleation and rapid growth of HKUST-1 islands were accompanied by the formation of a continuously nucleating but slowly growing HKUST-1 thin layer or ‘carpet’.
Microscopy can provide time-​resolved (seconds to minutes) and spatially resolved (down to atomic resolution) data on crystal growth as well as amorphous-​phase growth and prenucleation cluster dynamics. LCTEM and LPAFM are particularly powerful as they can operate in solution, enabling information on early stage dynamics and solid-​phase growth to be obtained directly.
Light scattering (timescale: seconds to minutes). Static light scattering (SLS) records the intensity of light scattered by a sample at a range of angles, whereas dynamic light scattering (DLS) records the fluctuations in scattering intensity over time. Both techniques are sensitive to scatterers of sizes $\geq1\mathrm{nm}$ , require homogeneous solutions and are a valuable tool for the study of early-​stage solution processes84. The utility of SLS and DLS in studying MOF early-​stage nucleation has been reviewed elsewhere51. Here, we focus on a newer light-​scattering technique: angle-​resolved second harmonic scatter­ing (AR-​SHS). This is a second-​order nonlinear optical technique that can simultaneously measure the size, shape and concentration of scattering species85. Additionally, polarizationresolved measurements enable the symmetry of the scattering species to be probed. To achieve adequate time resolution, a method has been developed of taking single-​shot measurements of the second-​harmonic signal at multiple angles by imaging a large portion of the AR-​SHS pattern on an electron multiplying charge-​coupled device camera in a Fourier-​imaging
