scheme — known as dynamic angle-​resolved secondharmonic scattering (dAR-​SHS)86. dAR-​SHS has been used to monitor the formation of ZIF-8 ( $\cdot\ensuremath{T_{d}}$ symmetry) with a time resolution of $3\:\mathrm{s}.$ , simultaneously measuring the size, shape and concentrations of intermediate species. dAR-​SHS provides symmetry information for individual molecules up to small crystallites; thus, the earliest stages of nucleation can be probed. The beauty of this technique is that detection of the scattered light requires no moving parts, making measurements quick and suitable for in situ monitoring of MOF formation.
QEXAFS (timescale: minutes). Although the techniques discussed thus far allow for the study of MOF nucleation and crystal growth on multiple length scales and timescales, they do not provide much local chemical information in the early complexation stages. By contrast, quick-​scanning extended X-​ray absorption fine structure (QEXAFS) does provide this information by directly probing changes in the coordination environment of metal centres as a function of time87. QEXAFS was used to study ZIF-7 crystallization with a time resolution of around 100 s (ref.88). When benzimidazole is added to a $N,N$ -​dimethylformamide (DMF) solution containing $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ , there is a shift in equilibrium from sixcoordinated $O_{h}Z{\bar{\mathbf{n}}}$ to four-​coordinated $T_{d}Z\mathrm{n}$ . Contrary to previous hypotheses, this increase in $T_{d}\mathrm{Zn}$ does not represent ZIF-7 formation, which is instead initiated by addition of the diethylamine modulator.
CLASSIC NMR (timescale: minutes to hours). Another relatively new technique, known as combined liquid-​state and solid-​state in situ crystallization NMR (CLASSIC NMR), also provides time-​resolved molecular-​level information89. CLASSIC NMR monitors both the solid phase and the liquid phase in a single experiment and can be carried out on a standard solidstate NMR spectrometer. This technique was used to study the nucleation and growth of MFM-500(Ni), particularly in the early stages of MOF nucleation90. Fitting the time-​resolved liquid-​phase $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ data to the Gualtieri model enabled the activation energies of nucleation $(61.4\pm9.7\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}})$ and growth $(72.9\pm8.6\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}})$ to be determined. Although solid-​state spectra were also obtained, they were not explored in detail because the liquid-​phase data proved more informative in this case.
In situ vibrational spectroscopy (timescale: milliseconds to minutes). Like QEXAFS and CLASSIC NMR, in situ vibrational spectroscopy also provides chemical insight into the early-​stage dynamics of MOF-​formation processes. The kinetics of HKUST-1 thin-​film growth were probed using in situ attenuated total reflection Fouriertransform infrared spectroscopy (ATR-​FTIR)91. The data revealed the formation of $(\mathrm{Zn,Cu)}$ hydroxy double salts from $\mathrm{{znO}}$ thin films and conversion of the hydroxy double salts into HKUST-1. Additionally, the flow-​cell apparatus used in this work can easily be applied to other solid or liquid reaction systems. Simultaneous in situ Fourier-​transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and in situ Raman spectroscopy were employed in investigating the formation mechanism of MIL- $.53(\mathrm{Al})^{92}$ .
Although inelastic neutron scattering can give the vibrational spectrum of a material without the selection rules of Raman or infrared spectroscopies13, the combined use of FTIR and Raman measurements allows all vibrational bands to be observed without the need for a neutron source. The authors identified a prenucleation building unit consisting of one linker and one Al atom and monitored the evolution of nuclei in solution through to final precipitation of the crystalline MOF phase. Combined in situ Raman and in situ FTIR spectroscopy was also used to investigate the mechanism of bifurcation of MIL-68(Al) and MIL-53(Al)93. In these experiments, MIL-68(Al) was favoured over MIL-53(Al) owing to a deficiency of terephthalic acid in solution and a slow MOF growth rate. Formic acid slowed prenucleation and crystal growth by forming hydrogen bonds with the carboxyl group of the terephthalic acid linker and not by competitive metal coordination.
Concluding remarks. MOF formation is a dynamic and complicated process occurring on different length scales and timescales. Thorough understanding of the whole process is achieved only with a combinational approach, whereby techniques operating in different space and time domains are used in tandem. X-​ray techniques are key for tracking crystal growth, while microscopy is useful for understanding local changes and surface phenomena. Insight into early-​stage, molecularlevel pheno­mena, such as solution-​phase dynamics and prenucleation cluster formation, is gained through NMR, QEXAFS and spectroscopic methods.
## Guest motion
The motion of guests throughout MOFs is of crucial importance to their potential applications from gas storage and separation to drug delivery and conductive membranes94–97. Herein, we review techniques that measure the position and translational diffusion of guests (Fig. 3), including the smallest possible guest: the proton.
PFG-​NMR (timescale: nanoseconds to milliseconds). Pulsed-​field gradient NMR (PFG-​NMR) (Fig. 3a) provides information on molecular translational diffusion with millisecond time resolution and on micrometre length scales98–100. Multinuclear magic-​angle spinning PFG-​NMR was employed to study the selectivity of ZIF-8 towards an ethene:ethane gas mixture101. Gas selectivity has typically been inferred from singlecomponent sorption isotherms, and studies investigating mixtures are still in the minority102. PFG-​NMR can directly investigate mixtures and provides more information than single-​component sorption isotherms. The study reports a $D_{\mathrm{ethene}}{:}D_{\mathrm{ethane}}$ (where $D$ is the diffusion coefficient) selectivity of 5.5, showing that ZIF-8 is an effective separation medium for this mixed gas stream. Activation barriers derived from this data showed that selectivity is dependent on guest size and almost independent of host–guest interactions. Considering guest– guest interactions, multinuclear magic-​angle spinning PFG-​NMR was used to study propene diffusion through ZIF-8 (ref.103). Propene diffusivity was independent of wavevectors, respectively; $2\theta$ is the scattering angle; $\sigma$ is the scattering cross section; $\varOmega$ is the solid angle; $S(\mathbf{Q},\omega)$ is the scattering function, where $\mathbf{\alpha}_{\mathbf{Q}}$ represents the scattering vector; and $\omega$ is the angular frequency. d | Interference microscopy (IFM) and infrared microimaging (IRM) measure transient guest concentration profiles across individual metal–organic framework (MOF) crystals. IFM has higher spatial resolution than IRM, with both using a computer-​controlled sample stage to map across individual crystals. e | Quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) probes guest adsorption and desorption kinetics by measuring the change in mass during guest uptake or release. f | Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measures electrical impedance to generate Nyquist plots, which are then used to derive sample ion and/or electron conductivity. ΔAbs, change in absorbance; $\widetilde\nu$ , wavenumber; FID, free induction decay; RF, radio frequency; $Z^{\prime}$ , real impedance; $Z^{\prime\prime}$ , imaginary impedance.
the presence of propane (up to 1:4 loading), contradicting observations in zeolites, in which the diffusivity of smaller species is impeded by the presence of larger species104. Framework flexibility might also have contributed to increased guest diffusion rates. Guests are not always ‘innocent’. In streams of $\mathrm{CH_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ PFG-​NMR showed that an increase in diffusion time led to a decrease in diffusion rate in ZIF-11 (ref.105); this is because the guest molecules bound to the pore walls deflect other molecules and prevent entry into the pores of the framework.
In 2018, PFG-​NMR was used to study the diffusion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in channel-​containing $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ (where dobpdc is $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -​dioxido- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -​biphenyldicarboxylate) in two crystallographic directions106. Surprisingly, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ diffusion perpendicular to the channels was $30\times$ slower than diffusion through the channels, but not zero. Given the kinetic diameter of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(3.3\mathring\mathrm{A})$ compared with the maximum pore size in the $a{-}b$ plane $(0.6\mathrm{\bar{A}})$ , the authors conclude that diffusion perpendicular to the channels occurs through defects, rather than by the framework distorting to allow $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ passage. Further considering the effects of pore and channel geometry, PFG-​NMR was used to measure the self-​diffusivity of different solvents in MOF-5 and IRMOF-3, which differ only by the addition of an amine group to the linker of the latter (benzenedicarboxylate versus 2-​aminobenzenedicarboxylate, respectively)107. It is possible to tune the self-​diffusivity of solvents linearly between the diffusivity in MOF-5 and IRMOF-3 by varying the linker mole fractions to obtain multivariate MOFs that contain both linkers. Intermolecular attractive forces between the MOF and guest had a surprisingly minor influence on the diffusion dynamics, with steric effects resulting from pore-​aperture size changes being the predominant factor.
More recently, PFG-​NMR was employed to examine the impact on guest diffusion of drastic phase changes in the highly flexible MOF DUT-49(Cu) brought on by increasing the loading of the guest108. DUT-49 has two main structures, open pore and closed pore, with vastly different guest-​diffusion properties. By using in situ PFG-​NMR supported by molecular dynamics simulations, the change from open pore to closed pore was observed as a change in $n$ -​butane diffusivity as the applied pressure of $n$ -​butane changed from $35.7\mathrm{kPa}$ to $37.4\mathrm{kPa}$ ; upon saturation of all pores with $n$ -​butane, the diffusivity decreased by fourfold. This work represents an innovative method for indirectly monitoring changes in MOF structure without directly following the changes in framework structure (such as by crystallographic methods).
The emergence of PFG-​NMR for measuring guest diffusion has revealed an apparent dominance of steric effects over host–guest and guest–guest electronic effects on the rates of guest transport in MOFs. Most studies have focused on characterizing the diffusion of industrially relevant gas streams, and a future challenge is to investigate more chemically complex guest molecules that give rise to host–guest and guest–guest interactions that define or even control molecular diffusion. Guest diffusion can be characterized with other techniques, such as quasi-​elastic neutron scattering (QENS; described below).
In  situ FTIR (timescale: milliseconds to minutes).
Monitoring the changes in the vibrational spectra (Fig. 3b) of guests as a function of time during diffusion provides complementary information to that obtained by PFG-​NMR measurements. The growth and decay of features during guest uptake and diffusion permits diffusivity calculations, whereas analysis of frequency shifts provides mechanistic insight into the diffusion mechanism and guest–host interactions109–113.
In situ FTIR has been used to monitor Fickian diffusion of $n$ -​butane in UiO-66 (ref.114). The intensity of infrared bands corresponding to free hydroxyl groups on UiO-66 decreased as infrared bands corresponding to hydroxyl groups interacting with $n$ -​butane grew. The diffusion coefficient for $n$ -​butane at various temperatures was measured and the activation energy for diffusion was calculated $(E_{\mathrm{a}}{=}21.0{\pm}1.2{\mathrm{kJmol}}^{-1}$ ). The diffusion-​limiting step was proposed to be dissociation of $n$ -​butane from the hydroxyl binding site on UiO-66. Benzene, toluene, $o$ -​xylene, $m$ -​xylene and $\boldsymbol{p}$ -​xylene diffusion in the same host have also been investigated, with the aim of establishing the entropic and steric effects on diffusion115. Using in situ FTIR, the diffusion coefficient was revealed to decrease in the order benzene $\approx$ toluene $>p$ -​xylene $>m$ -​xylene $>o$ -​xylene, again indicating that steric effects have a major role in diffusivity.
QENS (timescale: picoseconds to nanoseconds). QENS (Fig. 3c) is a neutron-​scattering technique in which the transfer of energy from the incident neutron to the analyte is small. When fitted to the appropriate Lorentzian function, QENS data yield, like PFG-​NMR, average particle displacements but with improved spatial and temporal resolution116,117.
The mechanism of benzene diffusion through MIL-47 and MIL-53 has been investigated using a combination of QENS and molecular dynamics118.
In flexible MIL-53(Cr), a guest-​induced transition from an open-​pore to narrow-​pore state is observed before benzene diffusion through a 1D hopping mechanism. Conversely, in rigid MIL-47(V), diffusion occurs through a tumbling, corkscrew-​like motion that is disfavoured in the flexible framework owing to higher activation energy barriers. This finding is in contrast to QENS data for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ diffusion in the same frameworks, which showed greater diffusivity in MIL-47(V), proposed to be due to stronger guest–host interactions in MIL- $53(\mathrm{Cr})^{119}$ .
The use of QENS in the analysis of MOF–guest interactions is not limited to unimolecular systems. Indeed, it has been used to investigate the co-​diffusion mechanism for a $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ mixture through UiO-66 (ref.120). Diffusion was different to that observed in zeolites121, in that inclusion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the MOF pores increased the rate of diffusion. The combined QENS and molecular dynamics data showed that inclusion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the MOF pores facilitated faster $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ diffusion by impeding attractive $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ –host interactions. This outcome is in direct contrast to the behaviours observed for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in ZIF-11, as measured by PFG-​NMR, for which the diffusion rate changes were ascribed to purely steric effects122. The metal nodes in both UiO-66 and ZIF-11 are coordinatively saturated, so this difference in behaviour is striking.
In 2019, a combined QENS and molecular dynamics study revealed a new type of non-​Fickian diffusion in MOFs, namely that diffusion of neopentane in MIL-47(V) occurs through the uncommon phenomenon of singlefile translation123. The authors suggest that this mecha­ nism can only be observed by QENS, leading them to speculate that the popular hopping mechanism described in previous studies might be single-​file diffusion that was mischaracterized by the chosen analytical method.
QENS is a powerful technique that can yield key mechanistic insights into guest diffusion in MOFs and is particularly useful in the analysis of proton conduction (discussed below).
Microimaging (timescale: seconds to minutes). Unlike QENS and PFG-​NMR, microimaging (Fig. 3d) by interference microscopy (IFM) or infrared microimaging (IRM) enables spatially resolved visualization of transient guest concentrations and guest fluxes, with time and space resolution of seconds and micrometres, respectively124–127.
IFM has primarily been used to study diffusion in zeolites128,129; however, in 2007, its use for measuring guest diffusion in MOFs was first demonstrated130. As the refractive index of MOF crystals is proportional to changes in local guest concentration, recording interference patterns affords maps of guest concentration. In this proof-​of-​concept study, the diffusivity of methanol in a manganese formate MOF remained constant from no-​loading to the pore-​filled state.
In IRM, changes in local guest concentration are proportional to changes in characteristic infrared bands of absorbing guest species, and although the spatial resolution of IRM is less than that of IFM, it provides chemical information that allows for the simultaneous measurement of multiple species131–134. IRM has increasingly been used to study either gas sorption and release in MOF single crystals135–137 or time-​dependent drug release from $\mathrm{MOFs^{138}}$ . For example, for streams of pure gases $\mathrm{(CO}_{2},\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ in ZIF-8, the host–guest and guest– guest interactions were shown to increase mobility at low loadings139. These increases disappear at high loadings or in a mixed-​gas stream, owing to the steric effects of clustering and pore-​window blocking. Contrary to the trends shown in $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ the diffusivity of alcohols initially decreases as loading rises from zero, as a result of hydrogen-​bonded guest-​cluster formation, until a minimum is reached. Beyond this loading, diffusivity then increases.
The IRM data from two related studies showed that ethane diffuses faster than ethene in ZIF-11, despite ethene being smaller140,141. It appears that ethene binds to ZIF-11, preventing linker rotation, restricting framework flexibility and decreasing the pore-​aperture size, in an example of electronic effects leading to an increase in steric blocking of guest diffusion. In a related mixed-​linker framework, ZIF-7-8, ethane diffusion is slower than in single-​linker framework ZIF-7 owing to a smaller pore aperture141. Notably, across a single crystal of ZIF-7-8, a wide range of diffusivities was observed. This range is to be expected in mixed-​linker MOFs, in which the composition can vary across the material, and this result showcases the benefits of non-​spatially averaged data attained by IRM.
IFM and IRM provide unique insights into guest diffusion in MOFs. In addition to the benefits described above, the transient concentration profiles obtained could be useful in tracking MOF formation, during which many species are present.
QCM (timescale: seconds). Changes in vibrational frequency of a piezoelectric quartz crystal under an applied AC current can be directly related to changes in mass on its surface142. Thus, quartz crystal microbalances (QCMs) (Fig. 3e) have been used for decades as precise mass balances with nanogram sensitivity143–146 and have recently been employed with MOFs for high-​precision sensing147.
In 2010, it was first demonstrated that QCM could be used to study diffusion in MOFs148. This method has since been applied to investigate cyclohexane uptake and diffusion in HKUST-1 (ref.149) and ferrocene diffusion in MOF thin films150,151. In the course of these studies, an elusive surface barrier effect was identified that can hinder guest uptake in MOFs. The surface barriers were caused by defects formed by exposure to air and/or humidity and could be removed by re-​immersion in the synthesis solvent152.
QCM is a powerful technique that can elucidate guest loading, diffusion coefficients and mechanistic detail. The technique is underused in the field of MOFs and deserves more recognition as an effective method for studying guest motion.
Proton conduction. The field of proton-​conducting materials is vast, and the application of MOFs for these purposes has been extensively reviewed153–155. As such, this section highlights just a handful of important cases in which the motion of hydrogen nuclei has been revealed through novel methods or in which an unusual behaviour has been demonstrated. Various techniques can be employed to study motion of this kind, such as electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS)156 (Fig. 3f), QENS and $^2\mathrm{H}$ solid-​state NMR, which help to elucidate diffusivity and mechanistic information157,158.
Proton conduction in MOFs usually occurs in one of two ways: a ‘Grotthuss’ mechanism with $E_{\mathrm{a}}{<}0.4\mathrm{eV}$ or by the vehicular mechanism with $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{a}}}{>}0.4\ensuremath{\mathrm{eV}}$ (refs159,160). A 2016 study identified an alternative mechanism of proton conduction in MOF MFM-500(Ni)161. QENS was used to elucidate the mechanism of proton motion as being ‘free diffusion inside a sphere’. The same mecha­ nism was reported in 2019 in a Ba-​based MOF, MFM-512 (ref.162). MFM-512 contains free carboxylic acid groups, which facilitate proton conduction through an extended hydrogen-​bonded network with water. At $99\%$ relative humidity, the related MFM-511, which does not contain free acid groups, had a proton conductivity of $5.1\times10^{-5}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ , whereas MFM-512 reported a conductivity of $2.9\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ , showing the importance of free acids in mediating moisture-​induced proton conduction.
As well as needing high proton conductivity, MOFs for applications in membrane materials for fuel cells163–165 and supercapacitors166,167 also require some form of conductivity control. Proton conductivity has been modulated using light in ${\mathrm{SSP@2IF}}{\mathrm{-}}8$ (where SSP is sulfonated spiropyran)168. The zwitterionic merocyanine phase of SSP, which predominates in the dark, forms a hydrogen-​bond network throughout the structure, facilitating proton conduction $(0.05\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ , which is just four times lower than that of commercial standard Nafion at $0.2\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}.$ ). When exposed to visible light, SSP isomerizes back to the neutral spiropyran form, breaking the extended hydrogen-​bond network and decreasing the conductivity by $28{,}000\times$ . As well as the high on/off ratio, $\operatorname{SSP}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ boasts exceptional recyclability with stability over 100 cycles and a rapid response time of 5 s. Several other recent studies confirm that spiropyrans and other photoactive moieties embedded in MOFs offer control over proton conductivity168–171.
A magnesium formate MOF with a reversible amorphous-​to-​crystalline structure change has been reported to exhibit on/off conductivity switching172. The wet material is crystalline and has a proton conductivity of $1\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ , but when the material dries it becomes amorphous and insulating. $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR was used to study the dynamics of proton transfer in the wet phase, and the mechanism was attributed to translational jump diffusion of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O^{+}}$ molecules, which are strongly restricted by the high activation barrier $_{(0.58\mathrm{eV})}$ .
In situ crystallographic methods were employed to directly measure proton conductivity173 in a MOF comprising 2D layers of ${\mathrm{Eu}}({\mathrm{III}})$ ions and phosphonate-​based linkers, connected by 1D $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ chains that form an extended hydrogen-​bonded network along the $c$ direction. Single-​crystal AC impedance revealed that protons conduct only along the direction of the extended 1D hydrogen-​bonded chains. Variable-​temperature single-​crystal X-​ray diffraction (SCXRD) combined with diffuse-​reflectance infrared Fourier-​transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) analysis showed that at high temperatures, the bound proton of $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ moves closer to the phosphonate $\mathrm{O^{-}}$ . Above a certain temperature, protons transfer from the $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ chains to the free phosphonate $\mathrm{O^{-}}$ , giving rise to a new crystallographic phase owing to evaporation of ${\mathrm{Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{NH}}$ . The methods outlined in this work will greatly aid the future design and analysis of proton-​conducting MOFs.
Protons are of course the smallest cations, but the conduction of larger ions in MOFs is a closely related developing field. Li-​ion conduction is of particular interest for emerging battery technologies. A 2018 report described the decoration of open metal sites in MOFs with $\mathrm{ClO_{4}^{-}}$ ions, which afforded biomimetic Li-​ion channels that can conduct $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ (ref.174). The highest conductivity was achieved by MIL-100(Al), which, after reacting with $\mathrm{LiClO}_{4},$ had a $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ conductivity of ${>}1\mathrm{m}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ , measured by EIS. In a direct comparison of MOFs UiO-66 and UiO-67, the latter displayed higher conductivity (0.65 versus $0.18\mathrm{mScm^{-1}}.$ ), attributed to larger pores facilitating efficient solvation of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and decreased confinement effects.
Moving to even larger ions, ionic liquid conduction in MOFs is another developing area175,176. Of particular relevance to this discussion is an example reported in 2019, wherein the loading-​dependent ion conduction of 1-​butyl-3-​methylimidazolium bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)amide was investigated in framework HKUST-1 (ref.177). Typically, nanoconfined fluids display their highest molecular transport rates at low loading when guest–guest interactions are minimal, with increased loading decreasing transport rates as guest–guest interactions become dominant. Ionic liquid transport in HKUST-1, recorded by EIS, displayed analogous behaviour until the highest loadings were reached, when an unanticipated further notable drop in ion transport was observed. Molecular dynamics simulations enabled the authors to attribute this third transport regime to a new ‘ion-​bunching mechanism’ in which cations and anions mutually block pores and clog the conduction pathways.
Concluding remarks. Several of the examples outlined above have used a combination of experiment and computation to understand guest diffusion, but the application of multiple experimental techniques to a single system is still limited. No single technique can probe the entire timescale necessary to monitor nonuniform diffusion from the nanoscale to the macroscale, but employing multiple methods could bridge the gap. For example, QCM could be used to interrogate the initial sorption kinetics and possible surface barriers of guest uptake into a polycrystalline MOF sample. The direct nature of QCM mass measurement provides the necessary time resolution to monitor mass changes in seconds, but slower-​to-​perform PFG-​NMR or QENS experiments would then be a better choice to provide information on fast guest diffusion within the body of the MOF. This is an example of how the time taken to measure a process is not the same as the timedependence of the process measured, highlighting the difference between measuring non-​equilibrium dynamic processes (initial gas uptake) and equilibrium situations (subsequent guest motion over nanosecond to millisecond timescales). Furthermore, it is important to consider the sample form. PFG-​NMR, QENS, in situ FTIR and QCM typically measure diffusion in bulk samples, such as powders or pressed pellets, whereas IFM and IRM study single crystals. Both cases have merit and provide complementary but different information. It is therefore advantageous to develop methods that permit the use of a technique on bulk material and individual crystallites, but as this is often difficult to achieve, again we encourage the combinational use of techniques that can probe guest behaviours in both many-​particle samples and individual crystals.
## Electron motion
Chemistry is, at the most fundamental level, described by the behaviour of electrons. In this section we focus on the motion of electrons in MOFs, specifically considering motion important to charge-​transfer processes. The majority of MOFs are not electrically conductive; however, if orbital overlap is favourable, electrons can flow through a framework. We review techniques that measure electron-​transfer processes within MOFs, providing insight into photoconductivity, excited-​state dynamics and charge transfer (Fig. 4). Such information underpins the development of electroactive MOFs for use as electrocatalysts178–181, rechargeable batteries and supercapacitors167,182–185, and conductive membranes in fuel cells97,186–188. Furthermore, the tunable and often crystalline nature of MOFs makes them ideal media for investigating the fundamental principles governing the flow of charge in 3D coordination space189.
Transient spectroscopy (timescale: femtoseconds to microseconds). Characterizing the excited states of electroactive MOFs is necessary for realizing their applications in solar cells190, fuel cells and artificial photosynthesis191. Pump–probe experiments (Fig. 4a) are well established in the study of excited-​state dynamics192,193 in gas, solution and solid states194–197, and typically proceed by exciting the system and then probing the transiently populated excited states using ultrafast laser set-​ups.
Optical transient absorption (OTA) and X-​ray transient absorption (XTA) are pump–probe techniques that differ only in the wavelength of the probe. The excited-​state and charge-​separation dynamics of ZIF-67 $(\mathbf{Co}^{2+})$ thin films have been explored using both techniques198. OTA revealed the relaxation dynamics of an exceptionally long-​lived excited state (with a lifetime $\tau$ of $2.9\upmu\mathrm{s}\dot{}$ ) formed after photoexcitation of the spin-​allowed $d{-}d$ transition of $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ in ZIF-67, shown by XTA to result from a picosecond-​timescale ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to ${{\mathrm{Co}}^{+}}$ reduction upon photoexcitation. Direct node-​to-​node communication was also demonstrated in ZIF-67 $(\mathbf{Co^{2+}})$ partially transmetallated with either $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ or ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions199. Incorporation of closed-​shell $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ into ZIF-67 impeded the formation of the excited charge-​separated state, characterized by OTA, but incorporation of redox-​active ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ facilitated charge transport from excited $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ centres, readily characterized by XTA. The ability to probe charge-​transfer processes directly not only offers the mechanistic insight necessary for photoenergy applications but also demonstrates how to spectroscopically interrogate MOFs in a way that underscores the advantages of combining multiple techniques.
These are, however, challenging experiments. The same combination of techniques was used to study photoexcitation of a ligand-​to-​metal charge-​transfer (LMCT) state in oleylamine-​stabilized nanoparticles of MIL-100(Fe) (ref.200). Scattering effects of the MOF colloidal suspension resulted in a weak OTA signal, but XTA showed that the lifetime of this LMCT state was approximately three orders of magnitude longer than that measured by OTA for a molecular reference complex.
When appropriately emissive components are present, ultrafast transient emission spectroscopy can also provide dynamic insight into excited-​state electron-​transfer processes in MOFs, especially when coupled with ultrafast OTA measurements. This technique was used to investigate the excited states of the compositionally identical but topologically different pyrene-​containing MOFs NU-901 and NU-1000 (ref.201). Solvent-​dependent picosecond-​timescale transient fluorescence spectra revealed that low-​lying polar excitonic states influence the excited-​state population decay process in NU-1000 more than in NU-901 because of their distinct topologies. In porphyrin-​based surface-​mounted zinc MOFs, it was possible to observe subpicosecond and picosecond $S_{2}{-}S_{1}$ internal conversion and decay processes, with strong quenching of both states when compared with the molecular analogue $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{TPP})$ (where
TPP is tetraphenylporphyrin) in ethanol202. This quenching was attributed to efficient energy transfer between adjacent chromophores in the MOF environment and is a function of the spatial preorganization that the framework provides.
In situ spectroelectrochemistry (timescale: seconds).
Continuing with spectroscopy, but moving to a longer timescale, in  situ spectroelectrochemistry (SEC) describes the combined use of spectroscopic and electrochemical techniques to follow redox processes204 (Fig. 4b). The technique requires specially designed electrochemical cells that permit intermediate species to be probed through spectroscopic techniques such as Raman and electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR), as well as ultraviolet (UV), visible and near-​infrared (NIR) absorption spectroscopy.
Using a combination of in situ SEC techniques (UV/ visible/NIR spectroscopy and EPR spectroscopy), it was identified that MOF $\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BPPTzTz})_{2}(\mathrm{tdc})_{2}\right]_{n}$ (where BPPTzTz is 2,5-​bis(4-(pyridine-4-​yl)phenyl)thiazolo[5,4-​d] thiazole and tdc is 2,5-​thiophenedicarboxylate) forms a mixed-valence state upon reduction, with intervalence charge-​transfer (IVCT) occurring over $3.80\mathring\mathrm{A}$ between cofacial thiazolo[5,4-​d]thiazole moieties205. Closely related IVCTs were similarly investigated in reduced forms of the isotopological frameworks $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(B D P P T z T z)_{2}(S D C)_{2}]}$ and $\mathrm{[Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{BDPPTzTz})_{2}(\mathrm{SDC})_{2}]$ (where SDC is 2,5-​selenophenedicarboxylate) using in situ EPR and visible–NIR $\mathrm{SEC^{206}}$ . The electron mobility of the $\mathrm{Cd^{2+}}$ analogue was about $3\times$ lower than that of the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\mathrm{MOF}$ because the larger size of the $\mathrm{Cd^{2+}}$ ions results in poorer band overlap between ligand pairs.
In situ Raman SEC was used in 2018 to monitor the redox-​dependent and pressure-​dependent chargetransfer properties of donor–acceptor MOF $\left[\left(\mathrm{Zn(DMF)}\right)_{2}\right.$ (TTFTC)(DPNI)] (where TTFTC is tetrathiafulvalene– tetracarboxylate and DPNI is $N,N^{\prime}$ -​di(4-​pyridyl)-1,4,5, 8-​naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide)207. This approach revealed the formation of a new electrochemically generated radical cationic complex in which charge transfer is heterogeneously distributed throughout the framework. Notably, the two-​point probe method was employed to study the bulk pressed-​pellet conductivity of this MOF — a property that is of rising interest in the MOF community.
By flipping SEC on its head and using light as stimulus rather than probe, it is possible to control the electronic conductance of a spiropyran-​loaded UiO-67 MOF thin film. Upon UV irradiation, the electronic conductance increased by one order of magnitude and was thermally reversible at room temperature208. Other photoswitches have also been used to impart conductivity control in framework materials. A not­ able example is a dithienylethene-​modified covalent organic framework film capable of switching on and off a light-​emitting diode in a circuit with the film by alternatingly irradiating with UV and visible light209. Light-​induced changes in film conductivity were characterized by a combination of optical spectroscopies and conductivity measurements. The interrogation of sample conductivity can be challenging, and hence we discuss promising time-​dependent methods next.
Conductivity measurements: TRMC and TRTS (timescale: femtoseconds to nanoseconds). The conductivity of solid samples can be measured either in bulk (typically a pressed pellet) or in a single crystal. EIS can be used to measure conductivity156; however, the measured value is strongly dependent on the type of contact method (for example, direct contact, wire-paste, in situ press or probe-paste methods), which can result in discrepancies of up to two orders of magnitude210.
An alternative is to use non-​contact methods, such as time-​resolved microwave conductivity (TRMC; Fig. 4a), which measures conductivity as a function of time (down to picosecond time resolution) without electrically contacting the sample. Free electrons absorb microwaves; thus, changes in the conductivity of a material can be determined from the changes in transmitted microwave power. In 2012, TRMC was used to measure the transient conductivity of semi-​conductive MOF $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{TTFTB})$ (where TTFTB is tetrathiafulvalene tetrabenzoate) and, in turn, to calculate its intrinsic charge mobility $(0.2\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})^{211}$ . The same technique was later used to study electron delocalization and charge mobility as a function of reduction in $\mathrm{K}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}$ $(0\leq x\leq2)$ (where BDP is 1,4-​benzenedipyrazolate)212. The study showed that fractional reduction of the MOF increased conductivity along one direction 10,000-​fold.
A similar technique, time-​resolved terahertz spectroscopy (TRTS) (Fig. 4a), measures transient conductivity by detecting changes in terahertz transmittance and, compared with TRMC, has increased time resolution (femtoseconds) owing to its higher frequency and shorter response times. TRTS was employed to investigate the conductivity and charge-​transport mechanism of a semiconducting $\pi{-}d$ conjugated MOF, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mathrm{THT})_{2}(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{3}$ (where THT is 2,3,6,7,10,11-​hexathioltriphenylene)213. TRTS measurements revealed the nature of charge transport as being Drude-​type, which was subsequently corroborated by Hall-​effect measurements.
TRMC and TRTS have no grain-​boundary effects or random orientation effects that plague contact methods, and there are no anisotropy issues. The field is in need of a standardized method for measuring local conductivity, and TRMC and TRTS may offer the solution.
Chronoamperometry (timescale: milliseconds to minutes). Electron and ion diffusion on the millisecond timescale can be studied by chronoamperometry (Fig. 4c), a time-​dependent electrochemical technique that measures a current versus time response. In 2020, chronoamperometry was used to independently measure the ion and electron diffusivities in ferrocene-​doped MOFs of increasing pore size (M $[\mathrm{OF-808<NU-1000<NU-1003}]$ ), finding that electron diffusivity decreased while ion diffusivity increased to a greater extent214. Thus, overall charge diffusivity increased with increasing pore size, ultimately being limited by ion diffusion.
Concluding remarks. The in situ and time-​resolved measurement of electron motion within MOFs is key to elucidating MOF electronic structure and function. Understanding charge-​transfer mechanisms, excitedstate dynamics and photoconductivity are essential for the design of electroactive MOFs. As with the study of MOF formation, a combined approach is again desirable to obtain as full a picture as possible, choosing complementary techniques for the specific length scales and timescales they are capable of probing.
## Framework motion
In this section, we examine the various techniques that can directly measure the motion of framework structural components. MOF flexibility is a hot topic in the field and has been previously reviewed, primarily from framework perspectives7,215–217. Numerous potential applications of dynamic MOFs rely on understanding how framework properties change after an applied stimulus. There are many types of framework motion, including gate-​opening, single-​crystal-​to-​single-​crystal transitions, breathing and swelling218–221. Here, we describe several time-​resolved or in situ approaches (Fig. 5) capable of probing these phenomena after application of stimuli, including changes in guest adsorption, temperature, pressure and light.
In situ XRD (timescale: seconds to hours). As most MOFs are crystalline, time-resolved and in situ XRD methods are often the method of choice for gaining structural insight into a dynamic event such as a phase transition or distortion. For example, in situ powder XRD (PXRD) was used to study $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC}\cdot\mathrm{TM})_{2}$ (DABCO) (where BDC-TM is 2,3,5,6-tetramethyl-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid and DABCO is 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane), which undergoes a reversible domino-lattice rearrangement upon water sorption222. The findings showed that even MOFs that retain their crystallinity and porosity upon moisture exposure can undergo notable molecular-​level structural changes owing to guest–host interactions. Similarly, in situ PXRD tracked a desolvation-triggered domino-​lattice rearrangement in AlTz-53 (ref.223). Within 40 s of activation, AlTz-53 undergoes a rever­ sible phase transition from a disordered, low-​porosity state (interconnected square-​lattice (sql) nets) to a highly crystalline one with high porosity (interconnected kagome $\bf(k g m)$ nets). This transition between distinct topological states was reversible over four cycles through activation and re-​immersion in polar solvents.
Through an arsenal of experimental and computational techniques, it was possible to unpick several factors affecting the probability of a MOF exhibiting negative gas adsorption: linker length, interpenetration, framework softness and mechanical stiffness224. In situ PXRD was used to analyse the micromechanical properties of the isoreticular MOFs DUT-46, DUT-48, DUT-49, DUT-50 and DUT-151 upon adsorption of guests. The authors discovered a new pressure-​amplifying framework in DUT-50, with even greater pore volume and larger pore size than DUT-49, and later reported that crystallite size has a pronounced effect on the negative-​gas-​adsorption behaviour of DUT-49 (ref.225) (Fig. 5a).
The nature of the solvent-​switchable continuous breathing mechanism in SHF-61 was investigated by means of in situ $\mathrm{XRD}^{226}$ . Continuous breathing differs from traditional breathing behaviour in that no phase transition appears to occur. In SHF-61, the mechanism is highly guest dependent. This behaviour offers the possibility of controlling guest uptake and selectivity, and indeed, gated $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake was achieved by partially solvating with DMF.
Some MOFs display negative linear compressibility, whereby contraction in one direction under hydrostatic pressure is accompanied by elongation in one or more directions227–229. A temperature-​induced positive correlation between negative linear compressibility and positive thermal expansion was demonstrated with the MOF [Ag(ethylenediamine) $\mathrm{]NO}_{3}$ (ref.230). Using variable-​temperature and variable-​pressure in  situ SCXRD, the mechanism for this direct coupling between negative linear compressibility and positive thermal expansion was presented as resulting from the voids ‘inflating’ and a framework ‘scissors-​opening’ motion.
It should be noted that in situ measurements are not time resolved if patterns are recorded at equilibrated pressures. There is an opportunity to gain more information by conducting true time-​resolved measurements at non-​equilibrated pressures. New detector technologies, such as hybrid pixel array detectors in use at the Diamond Light Source, can improve the acquisition time of XRD patterns, enabling truly time-​resolved XRD experiments231,232.
An inherent disadvantage of in situ XRD for the study of framework motion is the inability to resolve transient non-​crystalline states traversed during a given phase transition. Developments in pair-​distribution function capabilities and analyses have also been applied to framework characterization under dynamic stimuli and may offer a solution to interrogating amorphous states233. Returning to the limitations of more common and accessible X-​ray diffraction methods, however, XRD studies are often parallelized with complementary spectroscopic or microscopic experiments, as discussed below.
In situ optical and vibrational spectroscopies (timescale: milliseconds to minutes). Optical and vibrational spectroscopies can acquire molecular-​level information on MOF dynamics without requiring crystallinity8. The stability of MOF ${\mathrm{a-Mg}}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}$ has been studied as a function of hydrostatic pressure using in situ Raman and in situ FTIR spectroscopy234. The activated framework was stable up to a pressure of $2\mathrm{GPa},$ whereupon a singlecrystal-​to-​single-​crystal transition occurs. When the MOF was loaded with guests such as DMF or benzene, no single-​crystal-​to-​single-​crystal transition occurred. Extending into the infrared for examining framework structural changes, the thermally induced amorphization of ZIF-4 and subsequent recrystallization to ZIFzni was investigated with in situ synchrotron far-​infrared (FIR) spectroscopy235. By monitoring the vibrational changes of the $\mathrm{ZnN_{4}}$ tetrahedra, it was possible to predict the likelihood of thermal-​induced amorphization of a given ZIF.
Functionalization of UiO-66 with a vibrationally active probe and subsequent 2D infrared spectroscopic analysis yielded information on ultrafast framework dynamics, measuring the MOF structural elasticity236. Framework deformations with time constants of 7 ps and 670 ps were identified, with the MOF structural dynamics slowing substantially when DMF was introduced into the framework. Pump–probe femtosecond FTIR and 2D infrared were applied to investigate local hydrogen-​bonding and framework adsorbed $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (ref.259). e | Liquid-​phase atomic force microscopy data showing a reversible change in framework surface structure from a tetragonal unit cell to rhombic (where γ is a lattice vector angle) as a function of guest concentration over time261. f | Extended X-​ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) data analysis showing a unique deformation in the Ni-​based paddle-​wheel node of DUT-8(Ni) before (bottom) and after (top) ${\mathsf N}_{2}$ adsorption266. The blue lines are the measured data, and the pink lines are the fit. ${\mathfrak{g l}}$ Terahertz time-​domain spectroscopy (THz-​TDS) spectra (the solid and dashed lines correspond to the experimental spectrum and calculated spectrum, respectively) revealing collective vibrational modes responsible for the proton conductivity of MOF ZnTr (ref.272). Coloured bars represent distinct motion types for ${\mathsf{P O}}_{4}$ tetrahedra in the MOF. An example is also shown of one of the vibrational modes (Ta) that is strongly correlated to proton conductivity. T, temperature; XAS, X-​ray absorption spectroscopy. Panel a adapted from ref.225, CC BY. 4.0. Panel b adapted with permission from ref.237, American Chemical Society. Panel c adapted with permission from ref.246, American Chemical Society. Panel d adapted from ref.259, CC BY 4.0. Panel e adapted from ref.261, Springer Nature Limited. Panel f adapted from ref.266, CC BY 3.0. Panel g adapted from ref.272, CC BY 3.0.
dynamics of MIL-53(Al) loaded with benzonitrile, benzene, cyclohexane or phenyl selenocyanate237 (Fig. 5b). 2D infrared allows for the correlation of ultrafast changes in a hydrogen bond with the dynamics of the hydrogen bond itself (sub-​picosecond timescale), framework dynamics (picosecond timescale) and guest motion (longer than nanosecond timescale)238.
Structural photoresponsivity in MOFs has been previously reviewed32,239,240 so we provide only a few examples in this section. Azobenzene moieties are archetypal photoactive components, and a functionalized ‘shell’ containing these molecules on the surface of UiO-68 enables light-​gated guest diffusion241. In situ UV–visible diffuse reflectance spectroscopy showed an $86\%$ increase in the uptake of 1-​pyrenecarboxylic acid when the shell is in its open form ( $E$ -​azobenzene) compared with when it is in its ‘closed’ form (Z-​azobenzene). Later, reversible modulation of the separation factors of gas mixtures was demonstrated with a MOF membrane with photoactive azobenzene and bis(4-​pyridyl)ethylene groups directly incorporated into the struts242. In situ UV–visible spectroscopy showed that the separation factor of a $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ mixture could be reversibly switched between 21.3 and 43.7 owing to decreased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption when azobenzene is in the $E$ form. The transient isomerization of azobenzene within a MOF membrane can also be measured using in situ FTIR243. Spiropyrans, another common photoactive component, have also been investigated in MOFs, albeit only as guests or as pendent groups to either nodes or linkers and not with the spiro-​core as an intrinsic part of the bridge between nodes4,5,208. The photoisomerization rates of MOFs functionalized with spiropyran moieties grafted to the linkers can be modulated as a function of the framework structure to achieve complete photoconversion as well as photoisomerization rates comparable to those of free spiropyrans in solution244.
NMR (timescale: seconds to minutes). In situ $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ NMR was used to investigate gate opening in DUT- $8(\mathrm{Ni})^{245}$ . The pore opening of DUT-8(Ni) was characterized by a $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ NMR signal with a chemical shift in the range $225\mathrm{-}230\mathrm{ppm}$ , which exceeds that of liquid Xe outside the crystallites. $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ NMR was also used to study the structural transition in pressure-​amplifying DUT-49 (ref.246) (Fig. 5c), a type of framework that undergoes spontaneous desorption of gas during pressure increase in a defined temperature and pressure range. The transition, which corresponds to mesopore contraction, was observed at ${\sim}0.15$ bar at $200\mathrm{K}$ by an increase in the $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ chemical shift from $130\mathrm{ppm}$ to $230\mathrm{ppm}$ . This change in the chemical shift is indicative of increased wall–Xe interactions in the new closed-​pore state. Additionally, the coexistence of open-​pore and closedpore peaks at 0.66–0.88 bar suggests that the transition is a collective phenomenon occurring in larger domains or crystallites. A series of isoreticular DUT-49 MOFs were investigated using the same technique247. Using the difference between the chemical shift of adsorbed Xe and the chemical shift of bulk liquid Xe, the authors could estimate the mean pore size of these isoreticular MOFs.
In situ $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR is frequently used to investigate framework motion induced by carbon-​containing guests such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or hydrocarbons248–252. For example, $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR was used to examine the effect of framework flexibility on the uptake selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from a $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ mixture253. Selectivity factors were calculated based on shift ratios, showing that the flexible variant of DUT-8(Ni) is more selective than the rigid variant of DUT-8(Ni), suggesting that flexibility increases selectivity.
Neutron studies (timescale: picoseconds to minutes). Inelastic neutron scattering and QENS are primarily used to study guest binding and motion within $\mathrm{MOFs^{254-256}}$ . However, cooperative effects can give insight into the motion of the framework surrounding the guests257.
QENS was used to determine the transport diffusivity and self-​diffusivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in ZIF-7 at a loading of $1.3\mathrm{mmolg^{-1}}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and $225\mathrm{K}$ (ref.258). This data, in parallel with in situ SCXRD data and uptake isotherms, showed how ZIF-7 undergoes a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -​induced structural change owing to migration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in its non-​uniform pores rather than by the typical ‘gate-​opening’ adsorption mechanism. Another key technique is neutron powder diffraction. A major advantage of neutron powder diffraction over XRD techniques is that both $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $^2\mathrm{H}$ are strong scatterers of neutrons, and thus their position and thermal motion can be more readily determined. Neutron powder diffraction was used to monitor a phase transition occurring in two Prussian blue derivative frameworks259 (Fig. 5d). By varying the concentration of adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}.$ the thermal expansion coefficients of both frameworks were continuously and reversibly tuned from negative to positive values. These temperature-​dependent neutron powder diffraction experiments are not time-​resolved, as the temperature is equilibrated prior to recording each pattern, but the information obtained can shed light on time-​dependent processes.
Microscopy (timescale: seconds to minutes). As well as finding extensive use in the in situ investigation of MOF synthesis, in situ microscopy techniques have proved to be effective time-​resolved probes of framework dynamics260. For example, local surface changes in MOFs during guest absorption were studied in real time using LPAFM261 (Fig. 5e). The surface was highly guest-​responsive, deforming on a timescale of $10\mathrm{min}$ in response to changes in guest concentration, whereas the bulk crystal showed no lattice changes. This increased flexibility at the surface is speculated to be due to both dynamic coordination binding at the solid–liquid interface and host–guest interactions, with both practical and conceptual overlap with the above-​mentioned surface growth mechanisms of MOF-5 identified using $\mathrm{SEM}^{62}$ and the surface defect ‘healing’ studied using QCM experiments152.
The moisture- and temperature-​induced breathing behaviour of individual MIL- ${.53}(\mathrm{Cr})$ nanocrystals was monitored directly using environmental transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and computational simulations262. The breathing transition occurs upon adsorption of between 1 and $25\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules per unit cell and is reversible by heating–cooling cycling. Environmental TEM is an effective method for studying framework-​level changes occurring in individual MOF crystallites.
In recent years, microscopy techniques have proven themselves as valuable in situ characterization tools, but their use in studying framework motion is uncommon compared with other techniques such as XRD and NMR. The information gained from environmental TEM and LPAFM is complementary to that from more traditional techniques and there is great scope for further in-​depth study of framework dynamics.
XAS (timescale: minutes). Many studies have demonstrated the utility of $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -​ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) in gaining local structural information on the metal nodes in $\mathrm{MOFs}^{263,264}$ , but its use in situ during structural changes is limited to a handful of examples. In situ XAS was used to study the local electron structure around $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{d}{\mathsf{p a}})_{2}\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{\mathsf{-}}i$ (where dpa is dipyridyl acetylene) as a function of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake265. Cu $\mathrm{L}_{2,3}$ edge spectra show weak systematic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -​induced effects resulting from distortions of the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ coordination shell.
Going a step further, in situ extended X-​ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) was employed to assist in characterization of the guest-​induced phase transition in DUT- $8(\mathrm{Ni})^{266}$ . By examining the EXAFS spectra, changes in node geometry during the closed-​phase to open-​phase transition were explained (Fig. 5f). Although this study used EXAFS measurements only to support in situ XRD experiments across the transition, the analysis did elucidate a unique local deformation of the Ni-​based paddle-​wheel node that occurs between the closed-​phase and open-​phase states. A key area of improvement for EXAFS is the time resolution, which, in principle, could be increased to at least the level of QEXAFS (hundreds of seconds)87,88.
$T H z$ -​TDS (timescale: picoseconds). A technique deserving of more attention is terahertz time-​domain spectroscopy (THz-​TDS), which is used to measure the frequency of lattice vibrations in $\mathrm{MOF}\mathbf{s}^{267-270}$ . These collective fluctuations become static transitions when enough energy is supplied to the system. Typically, a frequency reduction is observed upon approaching a structural transition, and analysis of the changes in these THz modes provides insight into the transformation mechanism271. These ‘soft modes’ have been shown both in silico and through experiment to dictate many of the dynamic features of MOFs, such as proton conduction272 (Fig. 5g) and framework mechanochemical properties273–278. This research avenue is therefore of fundamental importance to the field of MOF dynamics.
Concluding remarks. The study of framework motion is essential for the design of stimuli-​responsive MOFs, and there are several experimental techniques that can achieve this. There is no lone technique that provides all the information necessary to understand long-​range crystallographic structural changes and molecular-​level local changes. Therefore, again, a combined approach is most effective.
## Conclusions and perspectives
We have summarized time-​resolved techniques used to study dynamic behaviours in and of MOFs, focusing on the utility of these techniques in the context of fundamental studies. Some approaches, such as XRD, TEM, SEM, AFM and NMR, are more frequently used than others. However, there are several methods that warrant more investigation, including QCM for studying guest behaviours, dAR-​SHS and CLASSIC NMR for the study of MOF formation, and TRTS and TRMC for the time-​resolved study of transient conductivity.
After a busy period of framework discovery, followed by increasingly elaborate framework design, the past decade has seen a rapid increase in the number of stimuli-​responsive, flexible MOFs. These materials hold much promise for future applications, but the time domain is the key to unlocking that promise. If these applications are to be realized, time-​resolved phenomena must be explored279. The methods discussed above represent the pioneers of such exploration.
Although not directly discussed herein, it is important to acknowledge the crucial role of computational methods in the future of the MOF time domain. Parallel development of computational methods is an integral part of the way forward, and we therefore refer readers to $\mathsf{R E F S}^{34,280}$ for an appraisal of the state and challenges of modelling stimuli-​responsive MOFs. An exciting new trend is the application of machine learning to understanding in the field, such as in predicting MOF synthesis conditions and MOF properties281,282. The synergistic relationship between computational and experimental methods is essential for the development of the field, just as, for example, time-​dependent density functional theory has become vital to the interpretation of the time-​resolved spectroscopic experiments that underpin much of our current understanding of the well established field of photocatalysis283,284. To that end, we recommend a 2021 review that summarizes the state of the art in modelling spatiotemporal processes in MOFs from the subnanometre to micrometre scale; this work highlights the need for a good understanding of realistic operating conditions and their interplay with computational modelling285.
There is growing appreciation for the time-​resolved investigation of MOFs, with the challenge having been laid down by Kaskel and colleagues in 2020 to design ‘4D-​MOFs’ with precisely engineered energy barriers between multiple states, and to improve terminology in the field, both qualitatively and quantitatively38. We have highlighted in this Review the technical methods by which the challenge can be met and the information those methods can already provide. Given that dynamic phenomena in MOFs happen on timescales from femtoseconds to hours, and on length scales from atoms to engineering rigs, no single method or technique can comprehensively solve all the questions that arise. We return, therefore, to Fig. 1, noting that ‘slow and macroscopic’ phenomenological analyses (such as nucleation and crystal growth in MOF formation, gas sorption dynamics and ion conductivity) are well served by existing methods. The ultrafast phenomena (such as excited-​state decay, photocatalysis and charge-​transfer dynamics) have been less thoroughly covered and remain primarily the domain of laser-​driven pump–probe experiments. Similarly, the phenomena that can be interrogated with truly atomic spatial resolution (including surface rearrangement on guest sorption and crystal growth at surfaces) are fewer, being largely limited to 2D or surface-​only experiments. The technical challenge is therefore to develop combinations of analyses or bring new techniques to the field that can bridge the time and space divide. Methods employed in other fields, such as tip-​enhanced Raman spectroscopy286, offer the possibility of combining atomic resolution with vibrational spectroscopy, and MOFs represent an ideal platform on which to push the current limits of such methods. We are also particularly excited to see how time-​resolved experimental data will be combined with computational methods to understand, design and generate new MOF structures with predictable stimuli-​responsive behaviours that lead to currently inconceivable applications.
